d2d1: always-on frame census — and two EndDraw optimisations measured DEAD

EndDraw was the #1 line in the cumulative profile and the standing plan was to skip its
GPU readback when nothing was drawn. Measuring first killed that idea and a second one,
for the cost of two runs instead of two implementations.

  skip readback when nothing was drawn      0.1% of EndDraws are empty (11 of 8,705)
  skip the acquire UPLOAD when the app
  clears the whole target first             2.1% clear-first (299 of 14,337)

⇒ Premiere redraws PARTIALLY into retained content, which is exactly why the time-ruler
fix had to add the seed upload in the first place. Both round trips are load-bearing.

⭐ AND EndDraw IS NOT WORTH CHASING ANYWAY. The July note ranked it #1 by CUMULATIVE
time, which is not the same as per-frame share:

    app draw                     33.0ms/frame
      EndDraw                     2.57ms   (289.6us x 3,556)   <- 7.8% of the frame
      BeginDraw                   1.27ms   (142.8us x 3,556)
      CreateWicBitmapRenderTarget 0.63ms   ( 71.3us x 3,552)
      top-8 entry points          8.1ms    <- the other ~25ms is diffuse

Driving EndDraw to zero moves 59.3ms/frame to 56.7ms: 18 fps -> 17.6 fps.

What the numbers DO show: those three lines are one object's lifecycle, and there are
~9 offscreen render targets per frame each paying ~500us of it. Acquiring one UPLOADS
the whole bitmap (seed_from_bitmap -> UpdateSubresource) and EndDraw reads it back --
an upload/download pair per target per frame, which is July's "36 GPU round-trips". The
lever is the NUMBER of targets, not the cost of one round trip, and that is Premiere's
choice rather than ours.

Ceiling worth knowing before anyone spends more here: the frame is 59.3ms of which
33.0ms is inside d2d1. Making d2d1 entirely free caps the timeline at ~38 fps.

The census stays: one unconditional increment per drawing call (and per Clear, which
mutates the target but does not go through neutron_op), reported only under
NEUTRON_D2D_PERF=1. ⚠️ Its reset is unconditional on purpose -- the neighbouring
per-frame reset is gated, and a counter that only resets under a diagnostic would report
every frame as non-empty.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
@@ -749,6 +749,8 @@
     float nc_l, nc_t, nc_r, nc_b;
     int nb_valid, nc_valid;
     unsigned nb_ops;
+    unsigned neutron_draw_ops;   /* NEUTRON: always-on drawing-call count since BeginDraw */
+    unsigned neutron_clear_first; /* NEUTRON: this frame's first op was a Clear */
     /* NEUTRON op-trace: did the app CLEAR this frame, and over what clip? A frame that
      * clears the panel and then draws almost nothing is the app wiping its own content --
      * not us erasing it. Reset with the unions at BeginDraw. */
--- a/dlls/d2d1/device.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/device.c
@@ -115,6 +115,15 @@
 /* NEUTRON op-trace: count one primitive by type for this frame. */
 static void neutron_op(struct d2d_device_context *context, enum neutron_op_id id)
 {
+    /* NEUTRON EMPTY-FRAME CENSUS: an ALWAYS-ON count of drawing calls since BeginDraw. The
+     * op-trace histogram above it is gated, so it cannot answer whether a production skip would
+     * ever fire. One increment per drawing entry point is nothing beside the call it accompanies.
+     *
+     * WHY: EndDraw is now the #1 cost (277us x 4,115 calls) and it is a CPU<->GPU readback sync.
+     * The standing proposal is to skip that readback when nothing was drawn -- but that is only
+     * worth building if such frames actually occur, which nobody has measured. */
+    ++context->neutron_draw_ops;
+
     if (neutron_optrace() && id < NEUTRON_OP_COUNT)
     {
         context->nb_hist[id]++;
@@ -3558,6 +3567,13 @@
 
     TRACE("iface %p, colour %p.\n", iface, colour);
 
+    /* ⚠️ A Clear MUTATES the target, so it counts as drawing. It does not go through neutron_op(),
+     * and without this a clear-only frame would look empty -- and any readback skip built on that
+     * would publish stale pixels. */
+    if (!context->neutron_draw_ops)
+        context->neutron_clear_first = 1;   /* NEUTRON: this frame opened with a Clear */
+    ++context->neutron_draw_ops;
+
     neutron_dr_add_clip(context); /* NEUTRON DIRTYRECTS: Clear fills the clip (or full RT) */
     if (neutron_dirtyrects() || neutron_dcdirect())   /* NEUTRON op-trace: record the wipe */
     {
@@ -3653,6 +3669,11 @@
 
     TRACE("iface %p.\n", iface);
 
+    /* ⚠️ Reset UNCONDITIONALLY -- the block below is gated, and a counter that only resets when a
+     * diagnostic is enabled would accumulate across frames and report every frame as non-empty. */
+    context->neutron_draw_ops = 0;
+    context->neutron_clear_first = 0;
+
     if (neutron_dirtyrects() || neutron_dcdirect()) /* NEUTRON: reset per-frame drawn/clip unions */
     {
         context->nb_valid = 0;
@@ -3701,6 +3722,28 @@
     if (context->target.type == D2D_TARGET_COMMAND_LIST)
         return context->error.code;
 
+    {
+        static unsigned empty, drew, seq;
+
+        static unsigned clearfirst;
+
+        if (context->neutron_draw_ops) ++drew; else ++empty;
+        if (context->neutron_clear_first) ++clearfirst;
+        /* ⭐ THE QUESTION THIS RUN ANSWERS. Acquiring a WIC target UPLOADS the whole bitmap to the
+         * GPU (seed_from_bitmap -> UpdateSubresource), so retained content survives a partial
+         * redraw. When the app's FIRST act is to clear the whole target, that upload is pure
+         * waste -- every pixel is about to be overwritten. ~9 targets per frame each pay an
+         * upload at acquire and a readback at EndDraw; killing the wasted uploads removes one GPU
+         * round trip per target per frame. Worth building only if apps really do clear first. */
+        if (neutron_perf() && ((seq++) & 0xFF) == 0)
+            ERR("neutron-emptyframe: empty=%u drew=%u (%.1f%% empty) | CLEAR-FIRST=%u of %u"
+                " (%.1f%%) target=%d %ux%u\n", empty, drew,
+                100.0 * empty / (empty + drew ? empty + drew : 1),
+                clearfirst, empty + drew,
+                100.0 * clearfirst / (empty + drew ? empty + drew : 1),
+                (int)context->target.type, context->pixel_size.width, context->pixel_size.height);
+    }
+
     if (context->ops && context->ops->device_context_present)
     {
         if (FAILED(hr = context->ops->device_context_present(context->outer_unknown)))
